    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 252\n\nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n\nAssembly Bill Number 9870, entitled:\n\n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the civil service law, in relation to the powers\n      and duties of the president of the state civil service commission\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  This bill would require the Department of Civil Service to  prepare  a\nnew  annual report that compiles input from each agency regarding alter-\nnative work schedules (AWS),  including  flexible  work  hours,  and  to\nconsider  how  specific  agencies  may  accommodate  and  implement such\nprograms.\n\n  I fully support the development and  evaluation  of  AWS  arrangements\nthat  balance  the  many  outside  demands  placed on an employee's time\nagainst the agency's operational  and  programmatic  needs.  Many  state\nagencies already have programs that promote and accommodate a variety of\nemployee  schedules,  including  flexible  hours. Thousands of employees\nalready avail themselves of the voluntary  reduction  in  work  schedule\n(VRWS)  program  which permits employees, with approval, to reduce their\nwork schedules to accommodate personal needs. The  Department's  Attend-\nance  and  Leave  Unit,  along  with  the  Governor's Office of Employee\nRelations, are available to  assist  in  the  development  of  such  AWS\narrangements  and  routinely  respond  to  agency requests for technical\nassistance.\n\n  In this instance, however, charging the Department with evaluating the\nfeasibility of such arrangements at every State agency and authority  is\nnot  realistic.  An  analysis of how and to what extent various agencies\ncould accommodate AWS and flexible hours would require a detailed under-\nstanding of each agency's operational needs and mission. Each agency  --\nand  even  individual  programs within each agency -- presents different\nstaffing needs,  nature  of  work,  hours  during  which  work  must  be\nperformed, and geographic limitations.\n\n  Further,  significant  effort  and resources would need to be diverted\nfrom the Department's core mission in order to collect and compile  data\nin  a  way that would provide meaningful information, analyze that data,\nand prepare an annual report. This belies the claim that the bill  would\nhave no fiscal impact on the State.\n\n  Based  on the impractical nature of the evaluation and reporting envi-\nsioned by the bill, and in light of  existing  sources  of  support  for\nagencies  that require technical assistance with the development, imple-\nmentation, and evaluation of AWS arrangements,  I  cannot  approve  this\nbill.  However,  I  am  directing the Department to work with the bill's\nsponsors and interested stakeholders to determine whether it is feasible\nto develop a list of best AWS practices across State  agencies,  and  if\nso, issue guidance for implementation.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 252,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2016
